@zetaab , I executed the steps you provided. I agree with @cmssczy, this failure message is not related to namespace naming.
I also got same mount error when I removed `-` from namespace;
```
Warning  FailedMount  37s (x3 over 38s)  kubelet            MountVolume.SetUp failed for volume "kube-api-access-dcxzs" : object "ghadevcloud"/"kube-root-ca.crt" not registered
```

Side note (while I'm looking at updatePodsInternal): the call to isPodInTerminatedState in this location may not make any sense but I'll have to walk through the reasoning later and double check.  The Kubelet config loop is not actually authoritative on the state of the pod, the pod worker is.  A pod can be listed at "completed" in the apiserver and the pod can still be happily running / setting up on the node.  In this case, it may not have relevance on this particular bug, but post the changes to make the pod worker correctly authoritative, I'm not certain that 

```
		if pm.secretManager != nil {
			if isPodInTerminatedState(pod) {
				// Pods that are in terminated state and no longer running can be
				// ignored as they no longer require access to secrets.
				// It is especially important in watch-based manager, to avoid
				// unnecessary watches for terminated pods waiting for GC.
				pm.secretManager.UnregisterPod(pod)
			} else {
				// TODO: Consider detecting only status update and in such case do
				// not register pod, as it doesn't really matter.
				pm.secretManager.RegisterPod(pod)
			}
		}
```

belongs here - it may need to move under the pod worker (who is the only component able to say when a pod no longer needs access to those assets). Certainly if I have a terminating pod with a long shutdown AND a long cleanup the watches can be cleaned up long before the pod reaches terminal state, which this logic can't guarantee because it's treating the apiserver as the source of truth instead of the pod worker.  If the apiserver for instance rejects a particular status update to set phase (unlikely but have seen variants before), the kubelet would keep hold of watches when it didn't have to.

I'll note that none of my changes addressed pods going into pod manager - so it's unlikely the registration and deregistration paths changed.  The only change upstream of that was admission related fixes and the pod is clearly admitted.

Going back to uid - this is wrong (but may not be the cause of this bug):

```
func (c *cacheBasedManager) RegisterPod(pod *v1.Pod) {
	names := c.getReferencedObjects(pod)
	c.lock.Lock()
	defer c.lock.Unlock()
	for name := range names {
		c.objectStore.AddReference(pod.Namespace, name)
	}
	var prev *v1.Pod
	key := objectKey{namespace: pod.Namespace, name: pod.Name}
	prev = c.registeredPods[key]
	c.registeredPods[key] = pod
	if prev != nil {
		for name := range c.getReferencedObjects(prev) {
			// On an update, the .Add() call above will have re-incremented the
			// ref count of any existing object, so any objects that are in both
			// names and prev need to have their ref counts decremented. Any that
			// are only in prev need to be completely removed. This unconditional
			// call takes care of both cases.
			c.objectStore.DeleteReference(prev.Namespace, name)
		}
	}
}
```

You cannot cache pods that the kubelet is aware of (podmanager is downstream of the config sources) by name/namespace because they are not guaranteed unique.  The Kubelet config loop guarantees only uniqueness of UID by source (which is a pretty giant footgun that we have to fix, i need to open an issue).  A static pod can change UID, have duplicate names/namespaces with other pods, etc.  So you have to key by both source and UID OR have a "first wins" based on UID.  

I doubt that's the cause of this bug, but realizing this and fixing static pods and regular pods was the source of a lot of issues after the refactor because a variety of safeguards were put at higher levels and just happened to accidentally mostly catch the problem.  We should just be sure that we don't have similar pod name/namespace/uids colliding here, or anything with static pod impact.

